<SubSection title="Transport in a Biomedical Context" id="Transport in a Biomedical Context">
 <p> Transport in biomedical context occurs at the sub-cellular, cellular, transcellular, tissue/organ, whole body, populations levels. 
Some examples include thermoregulation, metabolism, bioheat transfer, blood as oxygen carrier, membrane transport, and liquid diffusion in tissue.    
</p>
 <p> Understanding transport at these levels can help with the design and analysis of a wide range of clinical and bioengineering applications, such as:
<Itemize>
<Item> cryopreservation </Item>
<Item> laser ablation of tumors </Item>
<Item> drug dosing and delivery </Item>
<Item> dialysis machines </Item>
<Item> artificial organs </Item>
<Item> gene delivery </Item>
<Item> cryosurgery </Item>
</Itemize>
</p>
</SubSection>


